  "hamTitle": "Ham radio tips",
  "hamNoData": "Once weather is loaded, tips on antenna setup, operating and lightning safety will appear",
  "hamStorm1": "Thunderstorm: do NOT set up or operate antennas outdoors! Disconnect feed lines to avoid lightning surge damage",
  "hamStorm2": "If already set up, take it down promptly; switch to indoor repeater / HF listening and keep gear dry",
  "hamRain": "Precipitation: bring rain covers / dry boxes, seal connectors with tape or heat-shrink, keep feed lines drained",
  "hamCold": "Cold / snow: Li-ion capacity drops — carry spare batteries kept warm; watch SWR if ice forms on antennas",
  "hamWind": "Wind {w}: guy and secure antennas firmly; lower beams / long wires when packing up",
  "hamHot": "Heat {t}°C: stay hydrated; avoid long full-power transmissions that overheat your gear",
  "hamHumid": "Humidity {h}%: moisture hurts insulation and antenna efficiency; more VHF/UHF loss; keep connectors rust-free",
  "hamFog": "Low visibility ({v} km): drive carefully; fog can create ducts — try distant VHF/UHF contacts",
  "hamGood": "Great weather for operating! Try repeaters / simplex on VHF-UHF; HF ionosphere shifts in the evening",
  "hamWindExtra": "Wind {w}: still guy the antenna and stay safe in the field",
  "hamStorm3": "Lightning approaching: disconnect the antenna feedline from your rig, move it outdoors to a ground rod to bleed static, switch off and unplug mains power so surges cannot enter via AC or LAN; do not use outdoor antennas or corded phones",
  "hamStorm4": "Static crashes (QRN) surge around thunderstorms and HF noise floor rises; wait about 30 minutes after lightning stops before raising antennas and transmitting again",
  "hamExtreme": "Torrential/extreme rain: watch for flash floods, standing water and rockfall — never set up on riverbanks or low ground; add a drip loop where the feedline enters the wall",
  "hamGale": "Wind force {w}: do NOT climb towers or masts! Lower or lay down Yagis and long wires, and check guy ropes, anchors and mast stays",
  "hamIce": "Ice on antennas and feedlines raises SWR and adds ice loading: do not force full power, first check guy tension and wait until ice melts before normal operation",
  "hamFrost": "Below 0℃: lithium battery capacity drops sharply — keep spares warm in a pocket; guard against frostbite on hands and face, carry hand warmers",
  "hamHeat2": "Heat makes PAs and PSUs derate: lower power, shorten continuous transmissions and make sure there is proper ventilation",
  "hamDust": "Dust storm: fine sand in connectors and insulators causes leakage and noise — use dust caps; dry friction builds static, so ensure a good ground bleed",
  "hamAir": "Poor air quality: wear a mask outdoors and limit exertion; pollution films on antenna insulators add leakage noise, so clean the antenna afterwards",
  "hamDew": "Dew point spread only {d}℃ — air is near saturation: gear and feedlines may condense moisture; let equipment warm up and dry before powering on to avoid shorts",
  "hamUV": "UV index {u} (high): protect yourself from sunburn during field work — long exposure also ages coax jackets and cable ties quickly",
  "hamLowPressure": "Low pressure ({p} hPa): weather is becoming unsettled — for long field sessions keep an escape route and watch nearby warnings",
  "hamHighPressure": "High, steady pressure ({p} hPa): inversions form easily and VHF/UHF tropospheric ducting is possible — try beyond-line-of-sight direct or repeater contacts",
  "hamGrayLine": "You are in the sunrise/sunset grey line: 20/40m HF propagation peaks now — the golden window for long-haul DX",
  "hamNight": "D-layer fades at night: 80/40m absorption drops with lower noise — great for regional and nighttime long-distance work",
  "hamRainFade": "Heavier rain causes rain fade above 1.2GHz: for microwave and EME work, drop to a lower band or wait for the rain to ease",
  "hamShower": "Showers come and go quickly: bring a rain cover, watch the cloud movement, and stop transmitting before removing the feedline",
